# VERSION
v0.3.0

# Changelog
- v0.3.0
    - **Architecture documentation consolidation.** Replaced the duplicated V1/V2/split Atlas designs and temporary Sampling/Harness notes with one current V3 architecture document. The new design records the development/production lifecycle, six report-type profiles, model and PDF harnesses, read-only production-corpus boundary, Claim/graph pipeline, current implementation status and production-release gaps; dated validation evidence and the machine-readable candidate catalog remain explicitly non-production artifacts.
    - **Development-only field discovery.** Sampling is now an explicit development workflow used to discover and review one extraction profile per EastMoney feed (`stock`, `industry`, `macro`, `new_stock`, `strategy`, `morning_report`). Production configuration fails closed if sampling is enabled, the Atlas production API does not register sample routes, and the production Cthulhu build omits sample navigation; production only consumes approved field catalogs for full extraction.
    - **Read-only production-corpus sampling.** Development Atlas can use a separate PhoenixA report catalog and MinIO logical source for production PDFs while persisting every sample run, per-document JSON and category review only to development PhoenixA. MinIO credentials can be narrowly imported from Artemis `config-production.yaml`; the dedicated endpoint must be declared `read_only`, and operators must replace the current credential with a server-side read-only MinIO policy key. Feed counts come from PhoenixA `report_type` metadata rather than MinIO prefixes because `new_stock` (407 production records at audit time) shares the `stock/` object prefix.
    - **Pluggable multi-model sampling harness.** Added per-stage model chains with priority/balanced failover routing, per-provider multi-key pools, failure counters, cooldown circuit breakers, request-level provider provenance and transparent failover. Free extraction and review can share NVIDIA NIM (GLM-5.2, Nemotron 3.5 and optional Muse Glimmer), OpenRouter, GLM and local Ollama capacity through a generic OpenAI-compatible text adapter with provider-specific `extra_body`; adding accounts, keys or compatible models is configuration-only. A missing, rate-limited or retired free model now reduces capacity instead of disabling sampling. Provider success now requires stage-level JSON parsing and evidence validation, so an HTTP 200 response with an incompatible schema fails over instead of poisoning a run.
    - **OpenRouter free-router capacity.** Added `openrouter/free` as a separately configurable, schema-filtered sampling provider while keeping fixed/free/local fallbacks. Atlas records the actual routed model returned by OpenRouter, disables hidden reasoning for extraction/review token efficiency, and requires both provider parameter support and Atlas business validation before accepting random-router output.
    - **Free JSON field-discovery pipeline.** Each PDF first produces a model-authored, independently readable JSON without a candidate-field wrapper. Long documents use representative page map/merge, bounded token budgets, durable per-document checkpoints, cache reuse and safe recovery of complete values from truncated JSON. A separate cross-document reviewer generalizes paths into reusable `CORE`/`CONDITIONAL` fields, preserves evidence document/path links, rejects metadata and over-specific names such as year/EPS suffixes, and never promotes a one-document observation to `CORE`.
    - **Sampling business-quality guardrails.** Added deterministic semantic-family merging, source/path ownership checks, six-feed coverage gaps, no-document handling, per-category re-review/audit tools and a cross-category catalog builder with evidence grades and extraction profiles. Specific observations such as sludge throughput remain in the source JSON but are generalized to reusable operating metrics rather than becoming production field names.
    - **Diversity-first candidate and review batches.** Metadata candidate pools now read at least 80 reports per requested type before stratification so a small run does not repeatedly select only recent earnings notes. Four-document canaries are reviewed in one batch, and raw JSON paths/English snake-case keys are deterministically generalized into graph-oriented families such as research subject, chain position, upstream/downstream relations, technology, competition, capacity and financial forecasts rather than becoming production field names.
    - **Two-seed six-feed validation and deterministic catalog.** Validated 48 per-document free JSON results across two four-document rounds for all six production feeds, including an OCR-recovered scanned morning report. Added report-type noise filters, additional synonym families, business audit evidence, a checked-in candidate catalog/validation report, and a deterministic catalog mode that preserves every audited reusable family even when a free final-review model omits it. The catalog remains a development candidate, not an approved production schema.
    - **Gated document parsing harness.** Added page-level text quality assessment plus interchangeable HTTP layout/OCR and local RapidOCR/PyMuPDF parsers. Born-digital PDFs remain on the inexpensive pdfplumber path; only empty/sparse/chart-label-dominated documents escalate, representative OCR pages are bounded and serialized for home-machine RAM safety, and fallback output is accepted only when it improves usable text/research signals. OCR remains an optional dependency group rather than a production baseline; benchmark and canary scripts support later Docling/PP-StructureV3 A/B evaluation.
    - **Async run operations and review UI.** Added persisted async sample runs, task idempotency, progress/callback reporting, per-category/per-document PhoenixA records and Cthulhu pages for runs and raw free-extraction JSON review. Orphaned runs fail closed on restart and already-paid document JSON can be re-reviewed without rerunning PDF extraction.
    - **Predicate object-type reconciliation.** Discovery corrects generic model object types only when a canonical predicate name implies a more specific type, while retaining specific extracted types.
- v0.2.1
    - Added home-development Atlas configuration for Zhipu `glm-4.7-flash`, text-extracted PDF model calls, MinIO `atlas-dev` sample JSON review output, and live smoke tooling against `chaos-dev` PDFs.
    - Added retry/timeout handling for model HTTP calls, PhoenixA report-list compatibility with the deployed home service, sample-result persistence, and expanded tests for Zhipu adapters, runtime wiring, extraction validation, and MinIO sample storage.
- v0.2.0
    - Rebuilt Atlas as a research-report knowledge production and read-only intelligence service; removed the previous downloader, event fingerprinting, impact scoring, direct database access, arbitrary Cypher, RSS, web-clip, and legacy pipeline implementation.
    - Added an Artemis-style project skeleton and configuration loader while keeping Atlas-specific `knowledge_production`, `knowledge_store`, and `intelligence` domain boundaries.
    - Added whole-PDF extraction with in-memory pikepdf owner-permission removal, strict field definitions, a complete JSON Schema, page-range validation, evidence requirements, assertion classification, and full-output regeneration after malformed model responses.
    - Added separate model endpoints for a PDF-capable extraction gateway and provider-neutral structured chat adapters; Ollama is the initial agent provider and OpenAI-compatible paid providers can be configured without changing domain workflows.
    - Added broker/type-balanced, sample-driven report selection, predicate/concept discovery, Cthulhu review, and immutable semantic YAML publication. Unreadable samples remain in the usefulness denominator, and the bootstrap semantic version enables no report type for production.
    - Added SW2021-seeded Atlas Canonical Industry construction, bounded model-assisted EastMoney crosswalk batches, complete-coverage validation, final review, and semantic YAML publication.
    - Added document-level entity coreference, candidate reranking, exact/fuzzy alias resolution, A-share `security_registry` links, provisional global/non-listed entities, and persistent entity aliases.
    - Added governed Relation, Quantified, and Analyst View claims, including operating-capacity changes, evidence, polarity, assertion types, intra-document deduplication, all-claim truncation review gates, and fact-only graph projection.
    - Added resumable extraction runs: downstream failures reuse the validated extraction result instead of invoking the PDF model again; `force=true` explicitly bypasses completed and reusable results.
    - Added a read-only query planner, allowlisted PhoenixA tools, grounded-citation validation, and an evidence-bound company industry review agent.
    - Added Atlas HTTP APIs for discovery, semantic governance, crosswalks, extraction batches, controlled queries, and company reviews.
    - Added a cached base-image Docker layout, production configuration, health check, persistent semantic configuration volume, and `deploy/scripts/deploy_atlas.py` based on the Artemis deployment pattern without hard-coded credentials.
    - Added isolated unit coverage for PDF/MinIO adapters, model regeneration, discovery, crosswalks, entity resolution, claim quality, resumable report consumption, query safety, runtime wiring, and company reviews.
- v0.1.0
    - Review design of Atlas and make necessary adjustments.
- v0.0.1
    - Init atlas
